hazync#205 / GHOST_GAINS G3 — route BIP340 Schnorr verification through the bigint2 accelerator.

⛔ EXPERIMENTAL, OPT-IN, AND IT MOVES METHOD_ID. Applied only when HAZYNC_BIGINT2_SCHNORR=1.
⚠ Requires patches/0005 as well: it reuses hazync_ecmult_verify, which the bigint2-ecdsa feature
exports. Enabling this alone leaves the symbol undefined and the guest fails to LINK -- loud, at
least, unlike the other traps in this tree.

secp256k1_schnorrsig_verify computes rj = s*G + (-e)*pkj via
    secp256k1_ecmult(&rj, &pkj, &e, &s)
which is the IDENTICAL call shape patch 0005 replaces for ECDSA
    secp256k1_ecmult(&pr, &pubkeyj, &u2, &u1)
so the mapping is u1 <- s, u2 <- e (already negated on the line above), Q <- pk. No new Rust: the
same accelerator entry point serves both curves.

Everything else stays libsecp's literal code -- the challenge hash, the xonly pubkey load, the
infinity check, the y-parity test and the final x comparison. As with 0005 this does NOT preserve
wNAF/GLV, so the same equivalence-surface argument applies and no larger one.

Measured motivation: block 962,000 is only 1.81% taproot by input, but #139 makes ECDSA and Schnorr
diverge ~13.8x per verification, so Schnorr is a disproportionate share of what survives the
accelerator -- and it grows directly with taproot adoption, which matters far more for a
genesis-to-tip historic mode than for tip blocks today.

⚠ Second-order effect worth having: with both curves accelerated their costs CONVERGE, so the 13.8x
divergence that #190 exists to model shrinks and the chunk straggler improves for free.

